    34	Next, we prove the following lemma on enumerating connected sets with restrictions on its size and its neighbourhood's size. 
    35	
    36	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:ab-conn-sets-nbrs} 
    37	Given integers $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{N}$, the number of triples $(X,Z,Y)$ where $Z$ is a $(\alpha,\beta)$-connected set and $X \uplus Y = N(Z)$ is $3^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$ and the set of all such triples can be enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(3^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.
    38	\end{lemma}
    39	\begin{proof}
    40	We use a simple depth-bounded search tree algorithm for enumerating the required triples. Each node of the search tree is labelled as $(X,Z,Y)$ where $|Z| \leq \alpha$ and $|X \cup Y| \leq \beta$. A node $(X,Z,Y)$ is associated with a measure $\mu=(\alpha+\beta)-|Z|-|X \cup Y|$. 
    41	
    42	Guess a vertex $v \in Z$. The root of the search tree is $(X=\emptyset,Z=\{v\},Y=\emptyset)$ with measure $\alpha-1+\beta$. At a node $(X,Z,Y)$, we choose an arbitrary vertex $u \in N(Z) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ and branch into three possibilities: $u \in Z$ or $u \in X$ or $u \in Y$ resulting in three new nodes. This branching is clearly exhaustive and the measure drops by one in each of the three branches. The depth of the tree is at most $\alpha+\beta$ and the number of leaves is at most $3^{\alpha + \beta}$. The leaves $(X,Z,Y)$ where $X \cup Y = N(Z)$, $|Z|=\alpha$, $|X \cup Y|=\beta$ correspond to the required triples such that $v \in Z$. 
    43	
    44	Then, we run this branching algorithm for all choices of $v$. Thus, the total number of triples $(X,Z,Y)$ where $Z$ is a $(\alpha,\beta)$-connected set and $X \uplus Y = N(Z)$ is at most $3^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$.
    45	Further, the set of all such triples can be enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(3^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.\qed
    46	%To bound the number of nodes in the search tree, we associate a measure $\mu=(\alpha+\beta)-|Z|-|X \cup Y|$ to each node. In root node the parameter $\mu = (\alpha + \beta) -1$. Then at each node the parameter $\mu$ drops by 1. In the leaf node either $|Z| = \alpha$ or $|X \cup Y| = \beta$ or $\mu=0$. Return the leaf node instance $(X,Z,Y)$ where $X \cup Y = N(Z)$.
    47	\end{proof}
