    97	\subsection{Potential Witness Sets and Potential Prefix Sets}
    98	
    99	Intuitively, if $G$ is $k$-contractible to $P_\ell$ with the witness structure 
   100	$(W_1,W_2,\ldots,W_\ell)$ satisfying the required properties, 
   101	a potential $k$-witness set is a candidate for $W_i$ with 
   102	$2 \leq i \leq \ell-1$ and a potential $k$-prefix set is a 
   103	candidate for the set of vertices in a `prefix witness set'.  See Figure~\ref{fig:path} for an illustration. 
   104	
   105	\begin{definition}[Potential $k$-witness Set]
   106	\label{def:k-potential-witness-set} 
   107	%A connected set $W \subseteq V(G) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ is called a potential $k$-witness set of $G$ if |(N(W_i) \cap W_{i-1}) \setminus X | +  |W_i| + |(N(W_i) \cap W_{i+1}) \setminus Y| \le k + 3 - |X|-|Y|$ and $G - W$ has two connected components $C_X, C_Y$ containing $X$ and $Y$, respectively.
   108	A connected set $W \subseteq V(G) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ is called a potential $k$-witness set of $G$ if 
   109	$|(N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y) | +  |W|  \le k + 5 - |X|-|Y|$ and $G - W$ has two connected components
   110	$C_X, C_Y$ containing $X$ and $Y$, respectively.
   111	\end{definition}
   112	
   113	Note that every non-terminal witness set in a $P_\ell$-witness structure of $G$ is a potential $k$-witness set. Next, we define the notion of a \emph{potential $k$-prefix set}. 
   114	
   115	\begin{definition}[Potential $k$-prefix set]
   116	\label{def:k-nice-subset}
   117	For a potential $k$-witness set $W \subseteq V(G) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ with $C_X$ and $C_Y$ being the two connected components of $G - W$ containing $X$ and $Y$, respectively, the sets $S_X := C_X \cup W$ and $S_Y := C_Y \cup W$ are called the \emph{potential $k$-prefix sets} associated with $W$.
   118	\end{definition}
   119	
   120	Note that a potential $k$-prefix set is a non-empty connected set $S \subseteq V(G)$ that contains a potential $k$-witness set $W$ such that $G[S]- W$ is one of the connected components of $G - W$. 
   121	The number of potential $k$-prefix sets is twice the number of potential $k$-witness sets since every potential $k$-witness set is associated with two $k$-prefix sets. 
   122	However, we note that a $k$-prefix set $S$  
   123	can be associated with more than one potential $k$-witness set $W$.
   124	Formally, there may exist potential $k$-witness sets $W \neq W'$ such 
   125	that $S = C \cup W = C' \cup W'$,
   126	where $C$ and $C'$ are the connected components of
   127	$G - W$ and $G - W'$, respectively.
   128	
   129	We will describe a dynamic programming algorithm in which every entry of the dynamic programming table that we subsequently construct will be indexed by a triple consisting of a potential witness set, a potential prefix set and a non-negative integer that is at most $k$. Therefore, our algorithm's running time will crucially depend upon an efficient enumeration of all potential witness sets and all potential prefix sets. Targeting towards this enumeration, we have the following lemma.
   130	
   131	\begin{lemma}
   132	\label{lem:conn-sets} 
   133	For given integers $\alpha, \beta$, the number of connected sets $W \subseteq V(G)\setminus (X \cup Y)$ in $G$ such that $|W|=\alpha$ and $|N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y)|=\beta$ is $\calO^*(2^{\alpha+\beta})$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^{\alpha+\beta})$ time. %Further, the number of potential $k$-prefix sets in $G$ is $\calO^*(2^k-|X|-|Y|)$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^k-|X|-|Y|)$ time. 
   134	\end{lemma}
   135	\begin{proof}
   136	    We use a simple depth-bounded search tree algorithm for enumerating the required sets. First we guess a vertex $v \in V(G) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ with the interpretation that $v$ is in the connected set $W$ with the desired properties. The root of the search tree is labelled as $(\{v\},\emptyset)$ and in general each node of the search tree is labelled with a pair $(Z,Z')$ where $|Z| \leq \alpha'$ and $|Z'| \leq \beta'$ for some $\alpha', \beta'$ such that $\alpha' \leq \alpha$ and $\beta' \leq \beta$. With every node $N$ of the search tree with label $(Z,Z')$, we associate the measure $\mu(N)=\alpha+\beta-(|Z|+|Z'|)$. At each node $N$ labelled by $(Z,Z')$ with $\mu(N)>0$, we choose a vertex $u \in N(Z) \setminus (Z' \cup X \cup Y)$ and branch into two possibilities -- $u \in Z$ or $u \in Z'$ resulting in two new nodes with labels $(Z \cup \{u\}, Z')$ and $(Z,Z' \cup \{u\})$ respectively. This branching is exhaustive as the first branch considers the case when $u \in W$ and the second branch considers the case when $u \in N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y)$. As the measure drops by one in each of the two branches, the depth of the tree is at most $\alpha+\beta$ and the number of leaves is at most $2^{\alpha + \beta}$. The leaves $(Z,Z')$ where $Z' = N(Z) \setminus (X \cup Y)$, $|Z|=\alpha$, $|Z'|=\beta$ correspond to the required sets $W=Z$ such that $v \in W$. Now, we repeat this algorithm for each possible initial choice of $v$. Thus, the total number of connected sets $W$ with the required properties is at most $2^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$. Further, the set of all such sets are enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.\qed
   137	\end{proof}
   138	
   139	As every potential $k$-witness set is also a connected set $W \subseteq V(G)\setminus (X \cup Y)$ such that $|W|=\alpha$, $|N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y)|=\beta$ and $\alpha+\beta \leq k+5-|X|-|Y|$, we have the following corollary. 
   140	
   141	\begin{corollary}
   142	\label{cor:pot-int-sets}
   143	    The number of potential $k$-witness sets in $G$ is $\calO^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ time.
   144	\end{corollary}
   145	%, the number of potential  $k$-witness sets is $\calO^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ time.
