   155	\subsection{A Dynamic Programming Algorithm}
   156	
   157	Towards defining the entries in the dynamic programming table $\Gamma$, we define the notion of a \emph{valid index}.
   158	Informally, it is a tuple consisting of a potential $k$-witness set $W$, a potential $k$-prefix set $S$ associated with $W$, and an integer $k' \in \{0\} \cup [k]$.
   159	The value corresponding to this index is set to \true,
   160	if $G[S]$ is $k'$-contractible to a path (with desired properties) 
   161	and $W$ is one of its end witness set. Note that there are two types of potential $k$-prefix sets,
   162	one containing $X$ and the other containing $Y$.
   163	We differentiate between these two types by adding a subscript, i.e.,
   164	$S_X$ denotes a potential $k$-prefix set containing $X$
   165	and $S_Y$ denotes a potential $k$-prefix set containing $Y$. %\red{Hence, as a corner case, we allow valid index to be tuple corresponding to the first or the last witness set.}
   166	
   167	\begin{definition}[Valid Index]
   168	\label{def:valid}
   169	For sets $S_X, W \subseteq V(G)$ and $k' \in \{0\} \cup [k]$, the triple $(S_X,W,k')$ is a \emph{valid index} if 
   170	\begin{itemize}
   171	\item $W$ is a potential $k$-witness set,
   172	\item $S_X$ is a potential $k$-prefix set associated with $W$, and
   173	\item $k' \in \{0\} \cup [k]$ and $k' + |N(S_X)| -1 \le k$. 
   174	\end{itemize}
   175	Similarly, we define valid indices corresponding to $S_Y, W \subseteq V(G)$ and 
   176	$k' \in \{0\} \cup [k]$.
   177	\end{definition}
   178	
   179	\noindent The table $\Gamma$ has entries only corresponding to valid indices.  
   180	We define 
   181	\begin{align*}
   182	\Gamma[S_X, W, k']  = &\  \true\ \text{if and only if $G[S_X]$ is $k'$-contractible to a path with witness}\\
   183	& \text{structure $(W_1,\ldots,W_q=W)$ for some $q \ge 2$ such that $X \subseteq W_1$}
   184	\end{align*}
   185	\noindent and 
   186	\begin{align*}
   187	\Gamma[S_Y, W, k'']  = &\  \true\ \text{if and only if $G[S_Y]$ is $k''$-contractible to a path with witness}\\
   188	& \text{structure $(W'_1,\ldots,W'_p=W)$ for some $p \ge 2$ such that $Y \subseteq W'_1$}
   189	\end{align*}
   190	
   191	Note that the third condition in Definition~\ref{def:valid} insists that $k'$ and $k''$ are small enough to accommodate `future' edge contractions which are at least $|N(S_X)|-1$ or $|N(S_Y)|-1$ in number, respectively.  
   192	Next we describe the initialization of entries of $\Gamma$.
   193	We describe the algorithm for entries of type $\Gamma(S_X, W, k')$
   194	and a similar process is applicable for entries of type $\Gamma(S_Y, W, k')$. 
   195	First, all entries are set to \false. 
   196	Then, the algorithm uses Corollary~\ref{cor:pot-int-sets} 
   197	to enumerate all potential $k$-witness sets
   198	of a predefined size and neighbourhood size 
   199	(which we specify later).
   200	In this step, the algorithm enumerates all the subsets 
   201	that are potential candidates for either 
   202	$W_2$ or $W_{\ell - 1}$.
   203	For each such set $W$, let $S_X$ and $S_Y$ be the two
   204	potential $k$-prefix sets associated with $W$.
   205	The algorithm sets $\Gamma[S_X, W, k'] = \true$
   206	for every $|S_X| - 2  \le k' \le k -|N(S_X)|+1$.
   207	Similarly, it sets $\Gamma[S_Y, W, k'] = \true$
   208	for every $|S_Y| - 2  \le k' \le k-|N(S_Y)|+1$.
   209	The correctness of this initialization follows from the definition.
   210	
   211	We now describe how the algorithm updates the entries in $\Gamma$.
   212	%Consider an entry that is initialized to $\false$. 
   213	Unlike most of the dynamic programming algorithms, our algorithm does not 
   214	`look back' previously set $\true$ entries to update an entry that is set to \false\ in the initialization phase.
   215	Instead, it 
   216	checks entries $\Gamma[S_X, W, k']$ that are $\true$ and `looks forward' 
   217	and sets certain entries $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, A, k^{\circ}]$ to \true\ 
   218	where $S_X \subseteq S^{\circ}_X$, 
   219	$(N(W) \setminus S_X) \subseteq A$, and ${k' + |A| - 1} \le k^{\circ} \leq k - |N_{G-S_X}(A)|+1$.
   220	Intuitively, $A$ is a subset that can be appended to the 
   221	path witness structure of $G[S_X]$ as a new witness set to obtain a path witness structure of $G[S_X \cup A]$. 
   222	Hence, if we can obtain a path from $G[S_X]$ (with desired properties) by 
   223	contracting at most $r$ edges, then we can obtain a path from 
   224	$G[S_X \cup A]$ (with desired properties)
   225	by contracting at most $r + |A| - 1$ edges. Thus, $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, A, 
   226	k^{\circ}]$ can be set to \true. 
   227	Refer to Figure~\ref{fig:path} for an illustration. 
   228	
   229	\begin{figure}[t]
   230	\centering
   231	\includegraphics[scale=.75]{images/path-contraction-fpt-solution-size.pdf}
   232	\caption{(Top)
   233	$W_q$ is one of the potential $k$-witness sets and $S_X$ and $S_Y$ are the corresponding potential $k$-prefix sets. The objective is to determine whether $G[S_X]$ and $G[S_Y]$ admit path witness structures $(W_1, W_2, \dots, W_{q})$ and $(W'_1, W'_2, \dots, W'_p)$ such that 
   234	$X \subseteq W_1$, $Y \subseteq W'_1$, $W_q = W'_p$ and total number of edges contracted is at most $k$ 
   235	(Bottom) 
   236	$W_1$, $W_2$, $W_\ell$, $W_{\ell-1}$ (shaded sets) are identified during the initialization phase.
   237	For a potential $k$-witness set $W$ and the corresponding 
   238	potential $k$-prefix set $S_X$, the algorithm enumerates 
   239	all the possible sets $A$ that can be appended to a path witness structure of $G[S_X]$. 
   240	The witness set $W_q$, mentioned in the top figure, lies
   241	in the unshaded area. The idea is to expand $S_X$ until it contains $W_{\ell - 2}$. The sets with sizes $\beta_1, \alpha, a$ and $b$ corresponds to four internal witness sets (except in some corner cases) justifying the search for sets $A$ satisfying $\beta_1 + \alpha + a + b \le k + 6 - |X|-|Y|$.  
   242	}
   243	\label{fig:path}
   244	\end{figure}
