   413	\subsection{Correctness and Running Time Analysis}
   414	In the following lemma, we justify the steps 
   415	that updates the value of entries in the table.
   416	Once again, we argue correctness only for entries of type $\Gamma[S_X, W, k']$
   417	as the argument is identical for entries of type $\Gamma[S_Y, W, k']$.
   418	
   419	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:table-correct}
   420	Algorithm~\ref{alg:path-conn} correctly computes 
   421	$\Gamma[S_X, W, k']$ for every valid index $(S_X, W,k')$. 
   422	\end{lemma}
   423	\begin{proof}
   424	We prove that $\Gamma[S_X, W, k']$ is set to 
   425	$\true$ if and only if $G[S_X]$ is 
   426	$k'$-contractible to a path with witness structure 
   427	$(W_1,W_2,\dots, W_q)$ such that $X \subseteq W_1$ and 
   428	$W_q= W$. 
   429	
   430	The correctness of the initialization step
   431	follows from the fact that Corollary~\ref{cor:pot-int-sets}
   432	enumerates all $k$-potential witness sets.
   433	Also, once an entry is set to \true, it never changes its value.
   434	Hence, the algorithm sets $\Gamma[S_X, W, k'] = \true$
   435	in the initialization step, if and only if
   436	$G[S_X]$ is $k'$-contractible to a path (that is $P_2$)
   437	with witness structure $(W_1,W_2)$ such that 
   438	$X \subseteq W_1$ and $W_2= W$. 
   439	
   440	%~\pt{need justification?}
   441	We prove the correctness of the update phase by induction on $|S_X| + k'$.
   442	The base case corresponds to all the values updated in 
   443	the initialization phase.
   444	Suppose the algorithm 
   445	sets $\Gamma[S_X, W, k']$ correctly for every valid index 
   446	$(S_X,W,k')$ where 
   447	$|S_X| + k' \leq r$ and $r>0$. 
   448	Consider an entry $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, 
   449	k^{\circ}]$ where $|S^{\circ}_X| + k^{\circ} = r + 1$ and $k^{\circ} \leq k-|N(S^{\circ}_X)|+1$. 
   450	We show that 
   451	$\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, 
   452	W^{\circ}_X, k^{\circ}]$ is set to $\true$ if and only if 
   453	$G[S^{\circ}_X]$ is $k^{\circ}$-contractible to a path with witness structure 
   454	$(W_1,W_2,\dots, W_q)$ such that $X \subseteq W_1$ and 
   455	$W_q = W^{\circ}$. 
   456	
   457	$(\Rightarrow)$ In the forward direction, suppose the algorithm 
   458	sets $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ to \true.
   459	If $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ is set to $\true$ 
   460	in the initialization phase, then it is the correct value, as mentioned above. 
   461	%then $S^{\circ}_ = W^{\circ}$ and $k \ge k^{\circ} \ge |S^{\circ}| - 1$. 
   462	%In this case, $G[S^{\circ}]$ is $k^{\circ}$-contractible to the path with witness structure $(S^{\circ})$. 
   463	Consider the case when $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ 
   464	is changed from \false\ to \true\ during the update phase. 
   465	This update was done during the inspection of some \true\ entry $\Gamma[\widehat{S}_X, \widehat{W}, \widehat{k}]$. 
   466	Then, the following holds.
   467	\begin{itemize}
   468	\item $W^{\circ} \in \calA_{a}(\widehat{S}_X, \widehat{W})$ where $|N(\widehat{S}_X)| \leq a=|W^{\circ}| \leq k+1$.
   469	\item $S^{\circ}_X=\widehat{S}_X \cup W^{\circ}$ and $\widehat{S}_X \cap W^{\circ} = \emptyset$.
   470	\item $\widehat{k}+a-1 \leq k^{\circ} \leq k-|N(S^{\circ}_X)|+1$. 
   471	\end{itemize}
   472	It follows that $|\widehat{S}_X| = |S^{\circ}_X| - a$ and $k^{\circ} - a + 1 \ge \widehat{k}$.
   473	As $a \geq 1$, we have $|\widehat{S}_X| + \widehat{k} \le  (|S^{\circ}_X| - a) + (k^{\circ} - a + 1) = r+2(1-a) \le r$. 
   474	By induction hypothesis, $\Gamma[\widehat{S}_X, \widehat{W}, \widehat{k}]$ is correctly computed. 
   475	That is, $G[\widehat{S}_X]$ is $\widehat{k}$-contractible to a 
   476	path with witness structure $(W'_{1}, W'_{2}, \dots, W'_{p})$ such that
   477	$X \subseteq W'_1$ and $W'_{p} = \widehat{W}$. 
   478	Then, $(W'_{1}, W'_{2}, \dots, W'_{p}, W^{\circ})$ is a path 
   479	witness structure of $G[S^{\circ}_X]$ implying that $G[S^{\circ}_X]$ 
   480	is $k^{\circ}$-contractible to a path with desired property. 
   481	This concludes the proof for the forward direction of the claim.
   482	
   483	$(\Leftarrow)$
   484	In the backward direction, suppose $G[S^{\circ}_X]$ is 
   485	$k^{\circ}$-contractible 
   486	to a path with witness structure $(W_1,W_2,\dots, {W_q})$ such that 
   487	$X \subseteq W_1$ and $W_{q} = W^{\circ}$. 
   488	We show that the algorithm sets 
   489	$\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ to $\true$. 
   490	If $q = 2$, then because of the correctness of the initialization phase, $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ is set to $\true$. 
   491	Consider the case when $q \ge 3$. 
   492	Let $S_X = W_1 \cup W_2 \cup \cdots \cup W_{q-1}$
   493	and $k' = k^{\circ} - (|W_{q}| - 1)$. 
   494	Then, $G[S_X]$ is $k'$-contractible to a path 
   495	with witness structure $(W_1,W_2,\dots, W_{q-1})$
   496	such that $X \subseteq W_1$.
   497	We prove that the algorithm correctly sets 
   498	$\Gamma[S_X, W_{q-1}, k']$ to \true\ and 
   499	then show that while examining $\Gamma[S_X, W_{q-1}, k']$, 
   500	it sets $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ to $\true$. 
   501	
   502	We begin by showing that the triples $(S_X,W_{q-1},k')$ and 
   503	$(S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ})$ are valid indices. 
   504	Clearly, $W_{q-1}$ is a potential $k$-witness set and 
   505	$G[S_X]-W_{q-1}$ is one of the connected components of 
   506	$G-W_{q-1}$ that contains $X$.
   507	Further, $k' + |N(S_X)|-1 \leq k^{\circ} - (|W_{q}| - 1) + |W_q|-1 \leq 
   508	k-|N(S_X^{\circ})|+1$. 
   509	Hence $(S_X,W_{q-1},k')$ is a valid index. 
   510	Similarly, $W_{q}$ is a potential $k$-witness set and 
   511	$G[S^{\circ}_X]-W_{q}$ is one of the connected components of $G-W_{q}$.
   512	Thus, by this property and the assumption on $k^{\circ}$, it 
   513	follows that $(S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ})$ is a valid index. 
   514	Since $W_q \neq \emptyset$, we have $|S_X| + k' \le |S^{\circ}_X| - |W_q| + k^{\circ} - (|W_q| - 1) \le r + 2 \cdot (1 - |W_q|) \le r$.
   515	By induction hypothesis, we can assume that the algorithm 
   516	correctly sets $\Gamma[S_X, W_{q - 1}, k']$ to \true. 
   517	
   518	While examining $\Gamma[S_X, W_{q-1}, k']$, 
   519	the algorithm constructs $\calA_{a}(S_X, W_{q-1})$ for every 
   520	$|N(S_X)| \le a \le k + 1$.
   521	To complete the argument, it suffices to prove that $W_q \in \calA_{a}(S_X, W_{q-1})$ where $a = |W_q|$. 
   522	It is easy to verify that
   523	$W_q \subseteq V(G) \setminus S_X$,
   524	$N(S_X) \subseteq W_q$, and 
   525	$W_q$ is a connected set in $G$. 
   526	It remains to show that condition on the cardinality
   527	mentioned in the definition of $\calA_{a}$ is satisfied.
   528	As mentioned before, the algorithm tries to expand 
   529	$S_X$ till it includes all the witness sets before 
   530	the penultimate witness set $W_{\ell - 1}$, i.e.,
   531	till $S_X = W_1 \cup W_2 \cup \cdots \cup W_{\ell - 2}$.
   532	Hence, when the algorithm 
   533	is enumerating the possible sets $A$ (which are candidates for $W_q$)
   534	to expand $S_X$ that has $W_{q-1}$ as its last witness set,
   535	it is safe to assume that $q - 1 \le \ell - 3$.
   536	Now, by applying Equation~\ref{eq:expand-cardinality-bound} for
   537	$i = q - 1$, we get the desired bound.
   538	Hence, $W_q \in \calA_{a}(S_X, W_{q-1})$, and 
   539	algorithm sets $\Gamma[S^{\circ}_X, W^{\circ}, k^{\circ}]$ to \true.
   540	This concludes the proof of the backward direction
   541	and hence that of the lemma. \qed
   542	\end{proof}
