     1	In this section, we give improved \FPT\ and exponential-time algorithms for \textsc{Cycle Contraction}. Let $n$ denote the number of vertices in $G$. 
     2	
     3	
     4	\subsection{An FPT Algorithm}
     5	We show that \textsc{Cycle Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*((2 + \epsilon_{\ell})^k)$ time where $0 < \epsilon_{\ell}  \leq  0.5509$ is inversely proportional to $\ell = n - k$ using the algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} as a subroutine.  
     6	
     7	Let $(G,k)$ be an instance of \textsc{Cycle Contraction}. If $k \geq n-3$, then $(G,k)$ is a \yes-instance if and only if $G$ is $k$-contractible to a triangle (which can be determined in polynomial time). Hence, we may assume that $k \leq n-4$ and so we wish to determine if $G$ is contractible to $C_\ell$ where $\ell \geq 4$. We begin with two basic observations. 
     8	
     9	\begin{observation}
    10	\label{obs:sumofws}
    11	If $G$ is $k$-contractible to $C_{\ell}$ with witness structure $(W_1,\ldots,W_\ell)$, then $\sum_{i=1}^{\ell} (|W_i|-1) \leq k$.  
    12	\end{observation}
    13	
    14	\begin{observation}
    15	\label{obs:group-of-small-witness-set}
    16	If $G$ is $k$-contractible to $C_{\ell}$ where $\ell \geq 4$ with witness structure $(W_1,W_2,\ldots,W_\ell)$, then there is a set of three consecutive witness sets $W_i$, $W_{i+1}$, $W_{i+2}$ such that $|W_i|+ |W_{i+1}|+|W_{i+2}| \leq 3+ \frac{3k}{\ell}$. 
    17	\end{observation}
    18	\begin{proof}
    19	Suppose there is no set of three consecutive witness sets with the desired property. Then, for each $i \in [\ell-2]$, we have $|W_i|+ |W_{i+1}|+|W_{i+2}| > 3+ \frac{3k}{\ell}$. Further, $|W_{\ell-1}|+ |W_{\ell}|+|W_{1}| > 3+ \frac{3k}{\ell}$ and $|W_{\ell}|+ |W_{1}|+|W_{2}| > 3+ \frac{3k}{\ell}$. Summing up these inequalities leads to $3 \sum_{i=1}^{\ell} |W_i| > 3\ell+ \ell \frac{3k}{\ell}$ which in turn leads to $\sum_{i=1}^{\ell} |W_i| > k + \ell$. This leads to a contradiction by Observation~\ref{obs:sumofws}. \qed
    20	\end{proof}
    21	
    22	Observation \ref{obs:sumofws} immediately leads to a $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$-time algorithm for determining if $G$ is $k$-contractible to $C_{4}$ or not. 
    23	
    24	\begin{lemma}
    25	\label{lem:c4algo}
    26	There is an algorithm that determines if $G$ is $k$-contractible to $C_{4}$ or not in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$ time. 
    27	\end{lemma}
    28	\begin{proof}
    29	If $G$ has more than $k+4$ vertices, then by Observation \ref{obs:sumofws}, $G$ is not $k$-contractible to $C_{4}$. Subsequently, assume $|V(G)|\leq k+4$. We iterate over all possible colorings of $V(G)$ using two colors and for each coloring, we contract each of the connected components of the two monochromatic subgraphs into a single vertex and check if the resultant graph is $C_4$. If no coloring results in $C_4$, then $G$ is not $k$-contractible to $C_{4}$. The set of colorings that results in $C_4$ are the set of $C_4$-witness structures of $G$ each of which uses at most $k$ edge contractions. As there are $2^{k+4}$ such colorings, the overall running time is $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$. \qed
    30	%Generate all possible $2$-colourings of $G$. There are $2^{k+4}$ such possibilities. For each such $2$-colouring of $G$ contract the monochromatic edges and check if the resultant graph is a $C_4$ in polynomial time. In any one of $2$-colourings of $G$ result into a $C_4$ then $(G,k)$ is a \yes-instance of $C_4$ contraction. Otherwise $(G,k)$ is a \no-instance of $C_4$ contraction. Thus this algorithm takes $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$ time to determines if $G$ is $k$-contractible to $C_{4}$ or not and also it generates all $C_4$-witness structures of $G$ if such witness structures exist. For the $2$-colouring of $G$ that resulted into a $C_4$, $C_4$-witness structure is obtained by adding the vertices of each induced monochromatic component to same witness set.
    31	\end{proof} 
    32	
    33	
    34	Next, we prove the following lemma on enumerating connected sets with restrictions on its size and its neighbourhood's size. 
    35	
    36	\begin{lemma} \label{lem:ab-conn-sets-nbrs} 
    37	Given integers $\alpha,\beta \in \mathbb{N}$, the number of triples $(X,Z,Y)$ where $Z$ is a $(\alpha,\beta)$-connected set and $X \uplus Y = N(Z)$ is $3^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$ and the set of all such triples can be enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(3^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.
    38	\end{lemma}
    39	\begin{proof}
    40	We use a simple depth-bounded search tree algorithm for enumerating the required triples. Each node of the search tree is labelled as $(X,Z,Y)$ where $|Z| \leq \alpha$ and $|X \cup Y| \leq \beta$. A node $(X,Z,Y)$ is associated with a measure $\mu=(\alpha+\beta)-|Z|-|X \cup Y|$. 
    41	
    42	Guess a vertex $v \in Z$. The root of the search tree is $(X=\emptyset,Z=\{v\},Y=\emptyset)$ with measure $\alpha-1+\beta$. At a node $(X,Z,Y)$, we choose an arbitrary vertex $u \in N(Z) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ and branch into three possibilities: $u \in Z$ or $u \in X$ or $u \in Y$ resulting in three new nodes. This branching is clearly exhaustive and the measure drops by one in each of the three branches. The depth of the tree is at most $\alpha+\beta$ and the number of leaves is at most $3^{\alpha + \beta}$. The leaves $(X,Z,Y)$ where $X \cup Y = N(Z)$, $|Z|=\alpha$, $|X \cup Y|=\beta$ correspond to the required triples such that $v \in Z$. 
    43	
    44	Then, we run this branching algorithm for all choices of $v$. Thus, the total number of triples $(X,Z,Y)$ where $Z$ is a $(\alpha,\beta)$-connected set and $X \uplus Y = N(Z)$ is at most $3^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$.
    45	Further, the set of all such triples can be enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(3^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.\qed
    46	%To bound the number of nodes in the search tree, we associate a measure $\mu=(\alpha+\beta)-|Z|-|X \cup Y|$ to each node. In root node the parameter $\mu = (\alpha + \beta) -1$. Then at each node the parameter $\mu$ drops by 1. In the leaf node either $|Z| = \alpha$ or $|X \cup Y| = \beta$ or $\mu=0$. Return the leaf node instance $(X,Z,Y)$ where $X \cup Y = N(Z)$.
    47	\end{proof}
