# Path-contraction endpoint certificates (arXiv:2403.06290)

Status: all local gates pass, but this result is **not ready to ship**. The
required four-vendor calls all returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total
limit)`, so STEP 4 and the diagnostic G9 arms have no scored attempts. G9 passes
on its only remaining gated component, the answer-size and intended-effort caps;
the API errors are not counted as model failures.

| Profile field | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
| Native domain | combinatorics |
| Object regime | finite discrete |
| Computational core | graph |
| Certificate form | integer tuple: sorted endpoint pairs |
| Intuition | symmetry: find exceptions to a half-swap involution |
| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
| Reduction | paper-central, Section 5.2, Theorem 4 |

This is representational coverage through the paper's own reduction. The solver
is handed the exact clique-incidence data of a chordal split graph together with
its Boolean-vector representation; the search is the Orthogonal Vectors core of
Theorem 4, not an external convenience reduction.

## Problem and trust model

[Krithika, Kutty Malu, and Tale, *Revisiting Path Contraction and Cycle
Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06290) define an `H`-witness structure
in Section 2 as a partition into nonempty connected bags whose quotient is
exactly `H`. In the forward proof of Theorem 4, an orthogonal pair `x_i,y_j`
in the reduction's Boolean-vector instance gives four explicit bags forming a
`P4` witness structure of the constructed split graph.

An instance here asks for all of a fixed number of endpoint pairs. Each pair
deterministically expands to the proof's four bags. `verify` checks exact support
disjointness, constructs the graph, checks the partition, runs connectivity
searches inside every bag, checks all six quotient adjacencies against `P4`, and
checks the contraction budget. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.

Generation is inverse. It samples the endpoint vectors first, samples each
designated mate inside the endpoint's complement, and then adds only rows that
intersect every opposite-side row. Non-endpoint rows occur in pairs under the
coordinate-half swap. The local rejection tests prevent extra solutions; they
do not discover the already-chosen certificate. All rows have the same Hamming
weight, and planted and decoy rows have the same one-row marginal language.

## Why Track B

This cannot honestly be Track A. The paper gives an `O*(2^k)` general algorithm
(Theorem 1), cites an `O(nm)` chordal-graph algorithm immediately before
Theorem 4, and the visible source instance can always be solved by scanning all
`n^2` Boolean-vector pairs in `O(n^2 d)` time. At the hard preset that exact
reference scan succeeds on 8/8 instances, performs 640,000 Boolean coordinate
products per instance, and takes about 0.04 seconds per instance in the recorded
run.

The compact route notices that swapping the low and high 32-coordinate halves
is an involution. Every decoy row has its swapped mate on the same side; only the
four endpoint rows do not. Two hash-membership passes isolate those rows, after
which only their 4-by-4 cross-product is tested. This is 216 exact word
operations. Without the symmetry, the solver faces 10,000 shuffled cross-pairs.

The easy regimes deliberately avoided are target `P3` (polynomial time,
Introduction), planar Path Contraction (polynomial time, Theorem 3), and small
contraction budget `k` (FPT by Theorem 1). These instances use the fixed-target
`P4` setting of Theorem 4 and `k=|V(G)|-4`.

## Worked demo

For `make_instance(n=6, d=16, weight=5, target_count=2, seed=3)`, the complete
rendered data are:

```text
X: x0 0xa602  x1 0x03a4  x2 0x6091  x3 0x8889  x4 0x9160  x5 0x8988
Y: y0 0x3a40  y1 0xc026  y2 0x26c0  y3 0x403a  y4 0x01e1  y5 0xa00b
k = 26; return exactly two [X-index,Y-index] pairs.
```

The answer is `[[0,4],[1,5]]`: both hexadecimal support intersections are zero.

```python
>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4], [1, 5]])
(True, 'ok')
>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4]])
(False, 'expected exactly 2 pairs')
```

A person can solve the demo on paper by performing 36 short hexadecimal AND
checks. `render(inst)` contains the full graph and witness definitions, not just
the abbreviated data above.

