 succeeded in 1ms:
     1	# Path-contraction endpoint certificates (arXiv:2403.06290)
     2	
     3	Status: all local gates pass, but this result is **not ready to ship**. The
     4	required four-vendor calls all returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total
     5	limit)`, so STEP 4 and the diagnostic G9 arms have no scored attempts. G9 passes
     6	on its only remaining gated component, the answer-size and intended-effort caps;
     7	the API errors are not counted as model failures.
     8	
     9	| Profile field | Value |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    12	| Native domain | combinatorics |
    13	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    14	| Computational core | graph |
    15	| Certificate form | integer tuple: sorted endpoint pairs |
    16	| Intuition | symmetry: find exceptions to a half-swap involution |
    17	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    18	| Reduction | paper-central, Section 5.2, Theorem 4 |
    19	
    20	This is representational coverage through the paper's own reduction. The solver
    21	is handed the exact clique-incidence data of a chordal split graph together with
    22	its Boolean-vector representation; the search is the Orthogonal Vectors core of
    23	Theorem 4, not an external convenience reduction.
    24	
    25	## Problem and trust model
    26	
    27	[Krithika, Kutty Malu, and Tale, *Revisiting Path Contraction and Cycle
    28	Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06290) define an `H`-witness structure
    29	in Section 2 as a partition into nonempty connected bags whose quotient is
    30	exactly `H`. In the forward proof of Theorem 4, an orthogonal pair `x_i,y_j`
    31	in the reduction's Boolean-vector instance gives four explicit bags forming a
    32	`P4` witness structure of the constructed split graph.
    33	
    34	An instance here asks for all of a fixed number of endpoint pairs. Each pair
    35	deterministically expands to the proof's four bags. `verify` checks exact support
    36	disjointness, constructs the graph, checks the partition, runs connectivity
    37	searches inside every bag, checks all six quotient adjacencies against `P4`, and
    38	checks the contraction budget. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    39	
    40	Generation is inverse. It samples the endpoint vectors first, samples each
    41	designated mate inside the endpoint's complement, and then adds only rows that
    42	intersect every opposite-side row. Non-endpoint rows occur in pairs under the
    43	coordinate-half swap. The local rejection tests prevent extra solutions; they
    44	do not discover the already-chosen certificate. All rows have the same Hamming
    45	weight, and planted and decoy rows have the same one-row marginal language.
    46	
    47	## Why Track B
    48	
    49	This cannot honestly be Track A. The paper gives an `O*(2^k)` general algorithm
    50	(Theorem 1), cites an `O(nm)` chordal-graph algorithm immediately before
    51	Theorem 4, and the visible source instance can always be solved by scanning all
    52	`n^2` Boolean-vector pairs in `O(n^2 d)` time. At the hard preset that exact
    53	reference scan succeeds on 8/8 instances, performs 640,000 Boolean coordinate
    54	products per instance, and takes about 0.04 seconds per instance in the recorded
    55	run.
    56	
    57	The compact route notices that swapping the low and high 32-coordinate halves
    58	is an involution. Every decoy row has its swapped mate on the same side; only the
    59	four endpoint rows do not. Two hash-membership passes isolate those rows, after
    60	which only their 4-by-4 cross-product is tested. This is 216 exact word
    61	operations. Without the symmetry, the solver faces 10,000 shuffled cross-pairs.
    62	
    63	The easy regimes deliberately avoided are target `P3` (polynomial time,
    64	Introduction), planar Path Contraction (polynomial time, Theorem 3), and small
    65	contraction budget `k` (FPT by Theorem 1). These instances use the fixed-target
    66	`P4` setting of Theorem 4 and `k=|V(G)|-4`.
    67	
    68	## Worked demo
    69	
    70	For `make_instance(n=6, d=16, weight=5, target_count=2, seed=3)`, the complete
    71	rendered data are:
    72	
    73	```text
    74	X: x0 0xa602  x1 0x03a4  x2 0x6091  x3 0x8889  x4 0x9160  x5 0x8988
    75	Y: y0 0x3a40  y1 0xc026  y2 0x26c0  y3 0x403a  y4 0x01e1  y5 0xa00b
    76	k = 26; return exactly two [X-index,Y-index] pairs.
    77	```
    78	
    79	The answer is `[[0,4],[1,5]]`: both hexadecimal support intersections are zero.
    80	
    81	```python
    82	>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4], [1, 5]])
    83	(True, 'ok')
    84	>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4]])
    85	(False, 'expected exactly 2 pairs')
    86	```
    87	
    88	A person can solve the demo on paper by performing 36 short hexadecimal AND
    89	checks. `render(inst)` contains the full graph and witness definitions, not just
    90	the abbreviated data above.
    91	
    92	## Difficulty presets
    93	
    94	| Preset | n per side | d | Row weight | Required pairs | Candidate space | Status |
    95	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    96	| demo | 6 | 16 | 5 | 2 | 630 | hand-solvable; exact enumeration |
    97	| easy | 40 | 64 | 20 | 4 | `C(1600,4)` | oracle run blocked by quota |
    98	| medium | 70 | 64 | 20 | 4 | `C(4900,4)` | locally verified |
    99	| **hard** | **100** | **64** | **20** | **4** | **416,416,712,497,500** | provisional shipping preset |
   100	
   101	`escalate` increases the coordinate dimension while holding `n`, the number of
   102	witnesses, and answer length fixed. Thus it enlarges the mechanical haystack
   103	without lengthening the certificate.
   104	
   105	## Gate results
   106	
   107	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
   108	|---|---|---|
   109	| G1 | pass | 16/16 preset/seed instances; planted, compact, and exhaustive routes agree |
   110	| G2 | pass | 6 corruptions rejected with 6 distinct reasons |
   111	| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose/fences; JSON round-trip exact |
   112	| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density about `2.40e-15` |
   113	| G5 | pass | shipping density above; demo exact count 1/630; 2,048 restart trials measured |
   114	| G6 | pass | four attacks at 0/8; exhaustive reference and compact route both 8/8 |
   115	| G7 | pass | doubled `n=200` instance builds/verifies; candidate space increases |
   116	| G8 | pass | 80 invariant and 80 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
   117	| G9 | pass (caps) | 40 chars, 10 estimated tokens, 8 atoms, 216 intended operations; diagnostic arms unscored |
   118	
   119	## Oracle loop and G9 arms
   120	
   121	The transcript files were written by `scripts/harden.py`; every row is an API
   122	error and none counts as an attempt.
   123	
   124	| Arm/preset | Scored solved/attempts | Outcome |
   125	|---|---:|---|
   126	| bare / easy | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 quota errors; harness aborted |
   127	| structural / hard | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 quota errors; harness aborted |
   128	| placebo / hard | 0/0 | four HTTP 403 quota errors; harness aborted |
   129	
   130	Hinted-minus-placebo is undefined. No claim about model hardness or the value of
   131	the symmetry hint can be drawn until the key is replenished. The structural and
   132	placebo hints are both 111 characters; the structural hint names only the
   133	half-swap/two-cycle invariant and does not state an endpoint or procedure.
   134	
   135	## Use
   136	
   137	```python
   138	import json
   139	import gen_2403_06290 as g
   140	
   141	inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
   142	question = g.render(inst)
   143	answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + json.dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
   144	assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
   145	```
   146	
   147	After valid bare, structural, and placebo oracle runs, emit from the repository
   148	root with:
   149	
   150	```bash
   151	bash scripts/emit.sh 2403.06290 20 hard
   152	```
   153	
   154	## Caveats
   155	
   156	- A script solves every instance quickly. That is the reason for Track B, not a
   157	  hidden hardness claim. The successful half-swap route is disclosed as well as
   158	  the generic all-pairs reference algorithm.
   159	- The `0/200,000` result is for a uniform sorted four-subset of the `n^2` typed
   160	  endpoint pairs. It does not estimate a solver conditioned on the half-swap
   161	  insight.
   162	- The panel tests equal-degree outliers, greedy minimum intersection, 256 random
   163	  restarts, and same/reverse row alignment. The cited `O(nm)` chordal algorithm,
   164	  an ILP encoding, and an external SAT/SMT solver were not run. The exact
   165	  `O(n^2d)` source-problem scan was run.
   166	- Requiring several endpoint pairs composes several witnesses for one graph;
   167	  the paper's literal decision problem asks for one. This keeps the answer short
   168	  while making a structure-aware random guess negligible.
   169	- Decoys are conditioned to avoid extra orthogonal pairs. They share the same
   170	  fixed-weight one-row language as plants, but the intended two-cycle correlation
   171	  is deliberately construction-visible at the set level.
   172	- `canonical_key` uses 1-WL plus degree and common-neighbor multisets. It is
   173	  invariant under tested row, coordinate, side, and composed relabellings but is
   174	  not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form; rare non-isomorphic collisions
   175	  may be collapsed.
   176	- The module is standard-library-only; `gvlib` is unnecessary for this finite
   177	  bit-set family.
   178	- The present transcript files contain only quota-error records. Re-run all
   179	  three arms and update the literal G9 counts before submission.

