/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/path.tex:113:Note that every non-terminal witness set in a $P_\ell$-witness structure of $G$ is a potential $k$-witness set. Next, we define the notion of a \emph{potential $k$-prefix set}. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/path.tex:129:We will describe a dynamic programming algorithm in which every entry of the dynamic programming table that we subsequently construct will be indexed by a triple consisting of a potential witness set, a potential prefix set and a non-negative integer that is at most $k$. Therefore, our algorithm's running time will crucially depend upon an efficient enumeration of all potential witness sets and all potential prefix sets. Targeting towards this enumeration, we have the following lemma.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/path.tex:131:\begin{lemma}
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/path.tex:133:For given integers $\alpha, \beta$, the number of connected sets $W \subseteq V(G)\setminus (X \cup Y)$ in $G$ such that $|W|=\alpha$ and $|N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y)|=\beta$ is $\calO^*(2^{\alpha+\beta})$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^{\alpha+\beta})$ time. %Further, the number of potential $k$-prefix sets in $G$ is $\calO^*(2^k-|X|-|Y|)$ and these sets can be enumerated in $\calO^*(2^k-|X|-|Y|)$ time. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/path.tex:136:    We use a simple depth-bounded search tree algorithm for enumerating the required sets. First we guess a vertex $v \in V(G) \setminus (X \cup Y)$ with the interpretation that $v$ is in the connected set $W$ with the desired properties. The root of the search tree is labelled as $(\{v\},\emptyset)$ and in general each node of the search tree is labelled with a pair $(Z,Z')$ where $|Z| \leq \alpha'$ and $|Z'| \leq \beta'$ for some $\alpha', \beta'$ such that $\alpha' \leq \alpha$ and $\beta' \leq \beta$. With every node $N$ of the search tree with label $(Z,Z')$, we associate the measure $\mu(N)=\alpha+\beta-(|Z|+|Z'|)$. At each node $N$ labelled by $(Z,Z')$ with $\mu(N)>0$, we choose a vertex $u \in N(Z) \setminus (Z' \cup X \cup Y)$ and branch into two possibilities -- $u \in Z$ or $u \in Z'$ resulting in two new nodes with labels $(Z \cup \{u\}, Z')$ and $(Z,Z' \cup \{u\})$ respectively. This branching is exhaustive as the first branch considers the case when $u \in W$ and the second branch considers the case when $u \in N(W) \setminus (X \cup Y)$. As the measure drops by one in each of the two branches, the depth of the tree is at most $\alpha+\beta$ and the number of leaves is at most $2^{\alpha + \beta}$. The leaves $(Z,Z')$ where $Z' = N(Z) \setminus (X \cup Y)$, $|Z|=\alpha$, $|Z'|=\beta$ correspond to the required sets $W=Z$ such that $v \in W$. Now, we repeat this algorithm for each possible initial choice of $v$. Thus, the total number of connected sets $W$ with the required properties is at most $2^{\alpha + \beta} \cdot n$. Further, the set of all such sets are enumerated in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{\alpha + \beta})$ time.\qed
