/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/abstract.tex:28:%\red{Both these problems are well-known to be \NP-complete and in this paper we revisit these problems from two perspectives -- the first one focusing on improved parameterized algorithms and the second one on polynomial-time solvability in special graph classes. Our first set of results on improved parameterized algorithms are the following.}
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:3:Typical editing operations are vertex deletions, edge deletions, edge additions, edge subdivisions and edge contractions. In this work, we focus on editing a simple undirected graph by only performing edge contractions. Contracting an edge results in the addition of a new vertex adjacent to the neighbors of its endpoints followed by the deletion of the endpoints. One may view an edge contraction as ``merging'' its endpoints into one thereby making them indistinguishable. Any loops or parallel edges created in the process are deleted so that the graph remains simple. In graph contraction problems, given a graph $G$ and an integer $k$, the interest is in determining if $G$ can be modified into a graph that belongs to a specific family of graphs by a sequence of at most $k$ edge contractions. Most basic such graph families are paths and cycles. This brings us to the problems studied in this paper, namely, \textsc{Path Contraction} and \textsc{Cycle Contraction}. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:5:In \textsc{Path Contraction} and \textsc{Cycle Contraction}, the objective is to determine if a path or a cycle respectively can be obtained from the input undirected graph $G$ on $n$ vertices by a sequence of at most $k$ edge contractions. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:9:\defproblem{\textsc{Cycle Contraction}}{A connected undirected graph $G$ and an integer $k$.}{Can one contract at most $k$ edges in $G$ to obtain a cycle?}
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:11:\noindent Let $P_{\ell}$ and $C_{\ell}$ denote the path and cycle respectively on $\ell$ vertices. When viewed as vertex sets rather than graphs, $P_{\ell}$ and $C_{\ell}$ are referred to as induced $\ell$-path and induced $\ell$-cycle, respectively. Early results by Brouwer and Veldman~\cite{BrouwerV87} show that \textsc{Path Contraction} and \textsc{Cycle Contraction} are \NP-complete\ even when the target graph (which is a path or a cycle) has four vertices (i.e., when $k=n-4$). However, \textsc{Path Contraction} and \textsc{Cycle Contraction} are polynomial-time solvable when the target graph is a $P_{3}$ or a $C_{3}$~\cite{BrouwerV87}. In fact, Brouwer and Veldman~\cite{BrouwerV87} showed that \textsc{Path Contraction} is \NP-complete\  when the target path has $\ell$ vertices for every $\ell \ge 4$. Later, Hammack~\cite{Hammack02} showed that \textsc{Cycle Contraction} is \NP-complete\ when the target cycle has $\ell$ vertices for every $\ell \ge 5$. These results imply that \textsc{Path Contraction} and \textsc{Cycle Contraction} are \NP-complete\ in general. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:13:\textsc{Path Contraction} is one of the first graph contraction problems to be studied in the parameterized complexity framework. The most natural parameter is $k$ and Heggernes et al.~\cite{HeggernesHLLP14} showed that \textsc{Path Contraction} admits a kernel with at most $5k+3$ vertices and an \FPT\ algorithm running in $\calO^*(2^{k + o(k)})$ time. They also showed that \textsc{Path Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^n)$ time by a simple algorithm -- color the graph with two colors, contract the connected components in the monochromatic subgraphs and check if the resulting graph is a path. Later, Li et al.~\cite{LI201720} improved the size of the kernel to at most $3k+4$ vertices. While there is no improvement to the $\calO^*(2^{k + o(k)})$-time algorithm known till date, Agrawal et al.~\cite{AgrawalFLST20} gave an $\mathcal{O}^*(1.99987^n)$-time algorithm  breaking the $\mathcal{O}^*(2^n)$ barrier. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:19:\noindent Our first result is an algorithm for this problem using a dynamic programming formulation that crucially uses the notions of \textit{potential $k$-witness sets} (Definition \ref{def:k-potential-witness-set}) and \textit{potential $k$-prefix sets} (Definition \ref{def:k-nice-subset}). It is inspired by the exact exponential-time algorithm by Agrawal et al.~\cite{AgrawalFLST20} for \textsc{Path Contraction} and an \FPT\ algorithm by Saurabh et al.~\cite{SaurabhST20} for \textsc{Grid Contraction}. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:22:%    \item (Theorem~\ref{thm:path-ends-fpt-algo}) \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ time. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:27:running in time $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:34:%    \item (Theorem~\ref{thm:path-fpt-algo}) \textsc{Path Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{k})$ time. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:40:running in time $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{k})$.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:43:%Our first result is an algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction} with $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$ running time (Theorem~\ref{thm:path-fpt-algo}) . We show this result by giving an algorithm to solve the following Our algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} runs in $\mathcal{O}^*(2^{k-|X|-|Y|})$ time (Theorem~\ref{thm:path-ends-fpt-algo}) leading to an $\mathcal{O}^*(2^k)$-time algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction}. The algorithm is a dynamic programming algorithm that uses ideas entirely different from that of the algorithm by Heggernes et al.~\cite{HeggernesHLLP14} and crucially uses the notions of \textit{potential $k$-witness sets} (Definition \ref{def:k-pot-pen-wit-set} and Definition \ref{def:k-pot-int-wit-set}) and \textit{potential $k$-prefix sets} (Definition \ref{def:k-nice-subset}). 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:47:Our original motivation for focusing on \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} was to understand its applicability in designing faster algorithms for \textsc{Cycle Contraction} since deleting one vertex from a cycle results in a path whose endpoints are neighbours of the deleted vertex. As cycles have treewidth $2$, one may use Courcelle’s theorem to show the existence of \FPT\ algorithms for \textsc{Cycle Contraction} (see \cite[Chapter 7]{CyganFKLMPPS15} for related definitions and arguments). However, the resulting algorithm has huge exponential factors in the running time. Belmonte et al.~\cite{BelmonteGHP14} showed that \textsc{Cycle Contraction} admits a kernel with at most $6k+6$ vertices which was later improved to $5k+4$ by Sheng and Sun~\cite{SHENG201914}. This immediately results in a single-exponential time algorithm, i.e., an $\mathcal{O}^*(c^k)$-time algorithm for some constant $c>200$, for \textsc{Cycle Contraction} -- simply color the graph with two or three colors, contract the connected components in the monochromatic subgraphs and check if the resultant graph is a cycle. We describe a faster algorithm using the algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} as a subroutine. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:50:%    \item (Theorem~\ref{thm:fpt-algo-cycle}) \textsc{Cycle Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2 + \epsilon_{\ell})^k$ where $0 < \epsilon_{\ell}  \leq  0.5509$ and $\epsilon_\ell$ is inversely proportional to $\ell = n - k$. 
