/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:63:%We show that \textsc{Cycle Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2.5508^k)$ time (Theorem~\ref{thm:fpt-algo-cycle}). %To our pleasant surprise, it turns out that \textsc{Path Contraction With Constrained Ends} is also useful in solving \textsc{Cycle Contraction}. \todo{K:add}
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:65:\textsc{Cycle Contraction} is related to an important graph parameter, namely, \textit{cyclicity}. The cyclicity of a graph is the largest integer $\ell$ such that the graph is contractible to $C_\ell$. This parameter was introduced by Blum~\cite{Blum82} and ever since there have been efforts towards understanding the complexity of computing cyclicity. The result of Brouwer and Veldman~\cite{BrouwerV87} which shows that \textsc{Cycle Contraction} is \NP-complete\ even when the target cycle is $C_4$ proves that computing cyclicity is \NP-hard\ in general. This result led to the study of the problem on special graph classes. Hammack~\cite{Hammack99} showed that the cyclicity of planar graphs can be computed in polynomial time. Therefore, \textsc{Cycle Contraction} is polynomial-time solvable on planar graphs. However, the status of \textsc{Path Contraction} on planar graphs is open. We resolve this question by showing the following result.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:68:%    \item (Theorem~\ref{thm:path-planar}) \textsc{Path Contraction} on planar graphs is polynomial-time solvable.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:73:\textsc{Path Contraction} on planar graphs admits a polynomial-time
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:77:\noindent This result uses the \textsc{Cycle Contraction} algorithm on planar graphs as a subroutine to obtain a polynomial-time algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction}. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:79:On chordal graphs, while \textsc{Cycle Contraction} is trivially solvable (in linear time), the status of \textsc{Path Contraction} is interesting. Heggernes et al.~\cite{HeggernesHLP14} proved that \textsc{Path Contraction} is polynomial-time solvable on chordal graphs by giving an $\mathcal{O}(nm)$-time algorithm where $m$ is the number of edges in the input graph. In fact, they showed a stronger result -- every connected chordal graph $G$ can be contracted to $P_{dia(G)+1}$ where $dia(G)$ denotes the diameter of $G$ (the maximum number of edges in a shortest path between two vertices). As no graph $G$ can be contracted to a path with more than $dia(G)+1$ vertices, this result is the best possible. As graphs with treewidth $tw$ are $tw$-degenerate\footnote[2]{A graph is $d$-degenerate if every non-empty subgraph has a vertex of degree at most $d$} and $tw$-degenerate graphs on $n$ vertices have at most $tw.n$ edges, it follows that any graph of treewidth $tw$ on $n$ vertices has at most $tw.n$ edges~\cite{CyganFKLMPPS15}. Hence, the $\mathcal{O}(nm)$-time algorithm for \textsc{Path Contraction} on chordal graphs is also an $\mathcal{O}(n^2 \cdot tw)$-time algorithm where $tw$ is the treewidth of $G$. %This algorithm is an $\mathcal{O}(n^2 \cdot tw)$-time algorithm where $tw$ is the treewidth of the input graph. 
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:81:Fine-grained complexity aims at making finer distinctions between problems based on their resource (like time, space) requirement. A crucial difference between the fine-grained complexity program and the theory of \NP-hardness is that all \NP-complete problems form an equivalence class -- if one of them is polynomial-time solvable, then all of them are. In contrast, there are few equivalence classes known in the realm of fine-grained complexity. One such class contains the \textsc{Orthogonal Vectors} problem. In the \textsc{Orthogonal Vectors} problem, given two sets of $n$ $d$-dimensional boolean vectors $X=\{x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n\}$ and $Y=\{y_1, y_2, \ldots, y_n\}$, the objective is to determine if there is a pair of vectors $x_i \in X$ and $y_j \in Y$ that are orthogonal, i.e., $\langle x_i \cdot y_j \rangle= 0$. It is easy to see that \textsc{Orthogonal Vectors} can be solved in $\calO(n^2 d)$ time. The Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture~\cite{WilliamsY14} states that \textsc{Orthogonal Vectors} cannot be solved in $\calO(n^{2-\epsilon})$ time for any $\epsilon > 0$ when $d=\mathcal{O}(\log n)$. By giving a fine-grained reduction from \textsc{Orthogonal Vectors} to \textsc{Path Contraction} on split graphs (which is a subclass of chordal graphs), we show the following result.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/intro-long.tex:84:%    \item (Theorem~\ref{thm:chordal-lb}) \textsc{Path Contraction} on chordal graphs cannot be solved in $\mathcal{O}(n^{2-\epsilon} \cdot 2^{o(tw)})$ time under the Orthogonal Vectors Conjecture.
