/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/cycle.tex:98:\begin{theorem}
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/cycle.tex:100:\textsc{Cycle Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2.5191^n)$ time.
/tmp/paper2403.06290.EoncWS/src/cycle.tex:104:%\\Given an input graph $G$ on $n$ vertices. First determine if $G$ can be contracted to longest induced even cycle $C_l$ in time $\mathcal{O}^*(2^n)$ where $l \geq 4$. If this is a \no-instance then $G$ cannot be contracted to neither an even cycle nor an odd cycle. From the above observation if $C_\ell$ is the largest induced even cycle to which $G$ can be contracted to then $G$ can be contracted to either $C_\ell$ or $C_{\ell+1}$. Now determine if $G$ can be contracted to an odd cycle $C_{l+1}$. For that enumerate all $C_{\ell}$-witness structures in time $\mathcal{O}^*(2^n)$. For each such enumeration of $C_\ell$-witness structure $(W_1,W_2,\ldots,W_{\ell})$ of $G$, we need to go over each witness set $W_i$ whose size is at most $\frac{2n}{\ell+1}$ in polynomial time. Now by solving \textsc{2-Disjoint Connected Subgraphs} problem in time $\mathcal{O}^*(1.7804^{|W_i|})$ for each $W_i$, we can if determine $G$ can be contracted to an odd cycle $C_{l+1}$. The total running time is $\mathcal{O}^*(2^n \cdot 1.7804^{{\frac{2n}{\ell+1}}} )$. For $l \geq 4$, \textsc{Cycle Contraction} can be solved in $\mathcal{O}^*(2.5191^n)$ time.\qed
