## Caveats

- A script solves every instance quickly. That is the reason for Track B, not a
  hidden hardness claim. The successful half-swap route is disclosed as well as
  the generic all-pairs reference algorithm.
- The `0/200,000` result is for a uniform sorted four-subset of the `n^2` typed
  endpoint pairs. It does not estimate a solver conditioned on the half-swap
  insight.
- The panel tests equal-degree outliers, greedy minimum intersection, 256 random
  restarts, and same/reverse row alignment. The cited `O(nm)` chordal algorithm,
  an ILP encoding, and an external SAT/SMT solver were not run. The exact
  `O(n^2d)` source-problem scan was run.
- Requiring several endpoint pairs composes several witnesses for one graph;
  the paper's literal decision problem asks for one. This keeps the answer short
  while making a structure-aware random guess negligible.
- Decoys are conditioned to avoid extra orthogonal pairs. They share the same
  fixed-weight one-row language as plants, but the intended two-cycle correlation
  is deliberately construction-visible at the set level.
- `canonical_key` uses 1-WL plus degree and common-neighbor multisets. It is
  invariant under tested row, coordinate, side, and composed relabellings but is
  not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form; rare non-isomorphic collisions
  may be collapsed.
- The module is standard-library-only; `gvlib` is unnecessary for this finite
  bit-set family.
- The present transcript files contain only quota-error records. Re-run all
  three arms and update the literal G9 counts before submission; no oracle
  hardness claim is made here.
