 ## Problem and trust model
 
-[Krithika, Kutty Malu, and Tale, *Revisiting Path Contraction and Cycle
-Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06290) define an `H`-witness structure
-in Section 2 as a partition into nonempty connected bags whose quotient is
-exactly `H`. In the forward proof of Theorem 4, an orthogonal pair `x_i,y_j`
-in the reduction's Boolean-vector instance gives four explicit bags forming a
-`P4` witness structure of the constructed split graph.
+[Krithika, Kutty Malu, and Tale, *Revisiting Path Contraction and Cycle Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06290) define an (H)-witness structure in Section 2 as a partition into nonempty connected bags whose bag-adjacency graph is exactly (H). The forward proof of Theorem 4 constructs a split graph with independent sets (X,Y) and clique (Z=\{z_X,z_1,\ldots,z_d,z_Y\}). Every orthogonal pair (x_i,y_j) yields four explicit bags whose quotient is (P_4).
 
-An instance here asks for all of a fixed number of endpoint pairs. Each pair
-deterministically expands to the proof's four bags. `verify` checks exact support
-disjointness, constructs the graph, checks the partition, runs connectivity
-searches inside every bag, checks all six quotient adjacencies against `P4`, and
-checks the contraction budget. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+An instance asks for a fixed number of endpoint pairs. For each pair, `verify` expands the paper’s four bags, constructs the graph, checks the partition, checks connectivity in every bag, compares all six inter-bag adjacencies with (P_4), and checks the contraction budget. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
 
-Generation is inverse. It samples the endpoint vectors first, samples each
-designated mate inside the endpoint's complement, and then adds only rows that
-intersect every opposite-side row. Non-endpoint rows occur in pairs under the
-coordinate-half swap. The local rejection tests prevent extra solutions; they
-do not discover the already-chosen certificate. All rows have the same Hamming
-weight, and planted and decoy rows have the same one-row marginal language.
+Generation is inverse. Endpoint vectors are sampled first; each designated (Y)-endpoint is sampled inside the complement of its (X)-endpoint. Decoys are then sampled from the same constant-weight Hamming layer, conditioned to create no additional orthogonal pair. The certificate is carried through the final row shuffles; neither the exhaustive scan nor the compact solver is run to discover it.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-This cannot honestly be Track A. The paper gives an `O*(2^k)` general algorithm
-(Theorem 1), cites an `O(nm)` chordal-graph algorithm immediately before
-Theorem 4, and the visible source instance can always be solved by scanning all
-`n^2` Boolean-vector pairs in `O(n^2 d)` time. At the hard preset that exact
-reference scan succeeds on 8/8 instances, performs 1,081,600 Boolean coordinate
-products per instance, and takes 0.18 seconds per instance in the final recorded
-run.
+This cannot honestly be Track A. The paper gives an (O^*(2^k)) general algorithm (Theorem 1), cites an (O(nm)) chordal-graph algorithm before Theorem 4, and the displayed reduction instance is solvable by scanning all (n^2) cross-pairs in (O(n^2d)). At the shipping preset, the implemented exact scan succeeds on 8/8 instances, performs 1,216,800 Boolean coordinate products per instance, and averaged 0.077 seconds in the recorded self-test.
 
-The compact route notices that swapping the low and high 32-coordinate halves
-is an involution. Every decoy row has its swapped mate on the same side; only the
-four endpoint rows do not. Two membership passes isolate those rows, after which
-only their 4-by-4 cross-product is tested. This is 276 exact word operations and
-0.00018 seconds in the recorded implementation. Without the symmetry, the
-solver faces 16,900 shuffled cross-pairs.
+The compact route notices that swapping the two 36-coordinate halves is an involution. Every decoy row has its swapped mate on the same side; only the four endpoint rows do not. Two membership passes isolate those exceptions, after which only a (4\times4) cross-product remains. The counted route is (2n+t^2=276) exact word operations and averaged (5.1\times10^{-5}) seconds in the implementation. Without that symmetry, a no-tool solver faces 16,900 shuffled cross-pairs and 72 coordinates per comparison.
 
-The easy regimes deliberately avoided are target `P3` (polynomial time,
-Introduction), planar Path Contraction (polynomial time, Theorem 3), and small
-contraction budget `k` (FPT by Theorem 1). These instances use the fixed-target
-`P4` setting of Theorem 4 and `k=|V(G)|-4`.
+The generator avoids the paper’s easy regimes: target (P_3) is polynomial-time solvable (Introduction), planar Path Contraction is polynomial-time solvable (Theorem 3), and small (k) is handled by Theorem 1’s FPT algorithm. It uses the fixed-target (P_4) construction of Theorem 4 with (k=|V(G)|-4).
 
