     1	# Path-contraction endpoint certificates (arXiv:2403.06290)
     2	
     3	| Profile | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | graph |
     9	| Certificate form | integer tuple: sorted endpoint pairs |
    10	| Intended intuition | symmetry: exceptions to a coordinate half-swap involution |
    11	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    12	| Reduction | paper-central, Section 5.2, Theorem 4 |
    13	
    14	This is representational coverage through the paper’s own central reduction, not native coverage of arbitrary Path Contraction instances. The solver receives both the Boolean-vector incidence table and the chordal split graph it defines; the search core is the Orthogonal Vectors instance used in Theorem 4.
    15	
    16	## Problem and trust model
    17	
    18	[Krithika, Kutty Malu, and Tale, *Revisiting Path Contraction and Cycle Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06290) define an (H)-witness structure in Section 2 as a partition into nonempty connected bags whose bag-adjacency graph is exactly (H). The forward proof of Theorem 4 constructs a split graph with independent sets (X,Y) and clique (Z=\{z_X,z_1,\ldots,z_d,z_Y\}). Every orthogonal pair (x_i,y_j) yields four explicit bags whose quotient is (P_4).
    19	
    20	An instance asks for a fixed number of endpoint pairs. For each pair, `verify` expands the paper’s four bags, constructs the graph, checks the partition, checks connectivity in every bag, compares all six inter-bag adjacencies with (P_4), and checks the contraction budget. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    21	
    22	Generation is inverse. Endpoint vectors are sampled first; each designated (Y)-endpoint is sampled inside the complement of its (X)-endpoint. Decoys are then sampled from the same constant-weight Hamming layer, conditioned to create no additional orthogonal pair. The certificate is carried through the final row shuffles; neither the exhaustive scan nor the compact solver is run to discover it.
    23	
    24	## Why Track B
    25	
    26	This cannot honestly be Track A. The paper gives an (O^*(2^k)) general algorithm (Theorem 1), cites an (O(nm)) chordal-graph algorithm before Theorem 4, and the displayed reduction instance is solvable by scanning all (n^2) cross-pairs in (O(n^2d)). At the shipping preset, the implemented exact scan succeeds on 8/8 instances, performs 1,216,800 Boolean coordinate products per instance, and averaged 0.077 seconds in the recorded self-test.
    27	
    28	The compact route notices that swapping the two 36-coordinate halves is an involution. Every decoy row has its swapped mate on the same side; only the four endpoint rows do not. Two membership passes isolate those exceptions, after which only a (4\times4) cross-product remains. The counted route is (2n+t^2=276) exact word operations and averaged (5.1\times10^{-5}) seconds in the implementation. Without that symmetry, a no-tool solver faces 16,900 shuffled cross-pairs and 72 coordinates per comparison.
    29	
    30	The generator avoids the paper’s easy regimes: target (P_3) is polynomial-time solvable (Introduction), planar Path Contraction is polynomial-time solvable (Theorem 3), and small (k) is handled by Theorem 1’s FPT algorithm. It uses the fixed-target (P_4) construction of Theorem 4 with (k=|V(G)|-4).
    31	
    32	## Worked demo
    33	
    34	For `make_instance(n=6, d=16, weight=5, target_count=2, seed=3)`, `render` returns:
    35	
    36	```text
    37	Find endpoint certificates for path contractions in a chordal split graph.
    38	
    39	The graph G is defined as follows.  X={x0,...,x5} and
    40	Y={y0,...,y5} are independent sets.  Z={zX,z0,...,z15,zY} is a
    41	clique.  Each displayed row is a fixed-width hexadecimal integer.  Bit c means
    42	the coefficient of 2^c (the rightmost hexadecimal digit contains bits 0..3).
    43	Vertex xi is adjacent to zX and to coordinate vertex zc exactly when bit c of
    44	its row is 1.  Vertex yj is adjacent to zY and to zc exactly when bit c of its
    45	row is 1.  There are no other edges.  Thus G is a connected chordal split graph.
    46	
    47	For a proposed pair [i,j], define Z1 as the neighbors of xi in Z, Z2 as the
    48	neighbors of yj in Z, and Z3=Z\(Z1 union Z2).  The pair is valid exactly when
    49	the following four nonempty bags form a path-witness structure in the listed
    50	order:
    51	  W1={xi}
    52	  W2=Z1 union Z3 union (X\{xi})
    53	  W3=Z2 union (Y\{yj})
    54	  W4={yj}
    55	A path-witness structure means that every bag induces a connected subgraph,
    56	the bags partition all vertices, and two distinct bags have at least one edge
    57	between them exactly when they are consecutive in the displayed order.  Such a
    58	structure contracts G to the four-vertex path P4 using exactly k=26
    59	edge contractions.
    60	
    61	Return exactly 2 distinct valid pairs.  Indices are 0-based and lie in
    62	0..5.  Order within a pair is always [X-index,Y-index].  Sort the list of
    63	pairs lexicographically; repeats are forbidden.
    64	
    65	X incidence masks:
    66	  x0: 0xa602
    67	  x1: 0x03a4
    68	  x2: 0x6091
    69	  x3: 0x8889
    70	  x4: 0x9160
    71	  x5: 0x8988
    72	
    73	Y incidence masks:
    74	  y0: 0x3a40
    75	  y1: 0xc026
    76	  y2: 0x26c0
    77	  y3: 0x403a
    78	  y4: 0x01e1
    79	  y5: 0xa00b
    80	
    81	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON list of 2
    82	integer pairs sorted lexicographically.
    83	Example of the required syntax only: <answer>[[0,0],[1,1]]</answer>
    84	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    85	```
    86	
    87	The answer is `[[0,4],[1,5]]`.
    88	
    89	```python
    90	>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4], [1, 5]])
    91	(True, "ok")
    92	>>> verify(inst, [[0, 4]])
    93	(False, "expected exactly 2 pairs")
    94	```
    95	
    96	A person can solve the demo on paper by doing at most 36 short hexadecimal AND checks.
